    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you compare the thermal conductivity and heat dissipation rates of ceramic brake pads versus semi-metallic pads during high-speed deceleration cycles? I'm specifically looking at how heat soak affects the friction coefficient over sustained heavy braking periods.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads generally have higher thermal conductivity compared to semi-metallic pads. During high-speed deceleration cycles, ceramic pads typically dissipate heat more effectively, which can prevent excessive heat soak and maintain a consistent friction coefficient. However, the initial heat soak effect might be less pronounced compared to semi-metallic pads, which, while dissipating heat more slowly, may have a higher friction coefficient that can be maintained longer before dropping significantly. Semi-metallic material delivers the benefit of long-lasting friction but at the cost of temperature sensitivity and potential for longer recovery times after intense braking events.",
