    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If we see a sudden, unpredictable spike in brake temperature or a slight loss of pressure during a cycle that isn't accounted for by the thermal modeling, what are the most common mechanical or systemic culprits for that kind of erratic behavior? I'm trying to identify if it's a hardware failure or just a glitch in the sensor data.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unpredictable spikes in brake temperature or pressure loss can have various causes. Common mechanical issues include a failing master cylinder, leaks in the brake line, worn brake pads, or air trapped in the brake system. These conditions can cause inadequate lubrication, uneven pad wear, or reduced hydraulic pressure that results in erratic temperature and pressure readings. Additionally, contamination in the brake fluid compromises its boiling point and can cause erratic sensor data.\n\n\nSystemically, a problem with the Anti-lock Braking System (ABS) can manifest as pressure loss, as the system intermittently engages and disengages to prevent wheel lock-up. Faulty sensors or defective wiring in the ABS or brake-by-wire systems may also lead to erroneous data being recorded or acted upon.\n\n\nAccurate system diagnosis involves checking both hardware and software components, including brake fluid levels, seal condition, and sensor calibrations, to ensure the safety and responsiveness of the vehicle's braking system.",
      "source": "target",
